---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Hairline from '../components/Hairline.astro';
import BookletDownload from '../components/BookletDownload.astro';
import IngredientMark from '../components/IngredientMark.astro';

const lessons = [
	{
		id: 'ube',
		name: 'Ube',
		accent: 'ube' as const,
		lesson: 'Own your name',
		blurb:
			"A purple yam Filipinos have grown for thousands of years, and the world discovered about a decade ago. The boom happened abroad. Most of the value stayed there too.",
		href: '/ube',
	},
	{
		id: 'yuzu',
		name: 'Yuzu',
		accent: 'yuzu' as const,
		lesson: 'Regulate before you sell',
		blurb:
			"An aromatic citrus from Kochi Prefecture that Japan turned into a managed, protected, high margin export. Swiss readers will recognize the playbook immediately, because it is theirs.",
		href: '/yuzu',
	},
	{
		id: 'matcha',
		name: 'Matcha',
		accent: 'matcha' as const,
		lesson: 'Scarcity is structural',
		blurb:
			"Zen tea culture meeting a social media demand shock. Matcha shows what a demand surge does to an artisanal supply base with an aging workforce.",
		href: '/matcha',
	},
];

const accentText = {
	ube: 'text-ube',
	yuzu: 'text-yuzu',
	matcha: 'text-matcha',
};

const accentBorder = {
	ube: 'border-ube',
	yuzu: 'border-yuzu',
	matcha: 'border-matcha',
};

const comparisonRows = [
	{
		label: 'Origin',
		values: ['Philippines', 'China, then Japan', 'China, then Japan'],
	},
	{
		label: 'Who controls the name',
		values: ['Nobody yet', 'Japan, by registration', 'Contested'],
	},
	{
		label: 'Value capture at origin',
		values: ['Low', 'High', 'Under pressure'],
	},
	{
		label: 'Pressure point',
		values: ['Falling production', 'Aging growers', 'Five year replant cycle'],
	},
	{
		label: 'The lesson',
		values: ['Own your name', 'Regulate before you sell', 'Scarcity is structural'],
	},
];
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Home"
	description="A root, a fruit, a leaf. Three ingredients and the Asia behind them: ube, yuzu and matcha, in six cocktails."
>
	<!-- Hero -->
	{/* `min-h-full`, not `min-h-screen`. BaseLayout makes the body a one viewport
	    flex column with main taking the slack, so full here means the screen
	    minus the header. Measured against the whole viewport instead, the hero
	    centers its content a header's worth too low and drops the photo caption
	    onto the fold. It also inherits dvh from the body, so on iOS Safari it
	    tracks the visible area rather than the taller URL-bar-collapsed one.

	    The padding and gaps are smaller than the site's usual rhythm on purpose.
	    Content shorter than the container is centered, so the padding is
	    invisible in the normal case and only does anything once the content
	    would otherwise overflow. Buying headroom costs nothing until it is
	    needed. */}
	<section class="min-h-full flex items-center border-b border-rule">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-12 flex flex-col items-center text-center gap-6">
			<h1 class="font-label text-3xl sm:text-4xl text-bone tracking-[0.2em]">Asia Pours</h1>
			<p class="font-serif text-xl sm:text-2xl lg:text-3xl text-ash max-w-xl lg:max-w-2xl">
				A root, a fruit, a leaf. Three ingredients and the Asia behind them.
			</p>
			<nav aria-label="Ingredients" class="flex items-center gap-6">
				{lessons.map((item) => (
					<a
						href={`#${item.id}`}
						class:list={['font-label text-sm tracking-[0.15em] hover:opacity-75 transition-opacity', accentText[item.accent]]}
					>
						{item.name.toUpperCase()}
					</a>
				))}
			</nav>
			{/* No booklet download here. A solid brass button as the first thing a
			    reader meets asks for the transaction before making the case, which
			    reads as a landing page rather than as a piece of writing. The ask
			    belongs at the end, in the closing band, where it follows the
			    argument instead of preceding it. Removing it also gives the
			    photograph back the room it needs.

			    Sized against the fold: the hero including the header has to clear a
			    13 inch laptop, so the figure grows to 20rem rather than as far as it
			    would like to. */}
			<div class="w-full max-w-[17rem] sm:max-w-[20rem]">
				<Figure
					src="/images/hero.jpg"
					alt="A flat lay of a halved purple ube, a partially peeled yuzu, a bowl of whisked matcha and a bamboo chasen, arranged on dark slate."
					caption="Ube, yuzu and matcha, arranged for the shoot."
					aspect="1:1"
					priority
				/>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- The premise -->
	{/* The column grows and the type grows with it. Widening alone would push
	    the measure past the point where the eye loses the start of the next
	    line, so `lg` buys 48rem of column but spends it at 18px rather than
	    16px, which keeps the line around 75 characters, the usual upper bound.
	    A large display gets a bigger page, not a longer line. */}
	<section class="mx-auto max-w-2xl lg:max-w-3xl px-6 py-20 lg:py-24 flex flex-col gap-6">
		<p class="text-base lg:text-lg leading-relaxed text-bone">
			You can try to read your way into a culture on the flight to Singapore. I found out the
			easier way is to order well. A drink is small enough to finish and specific enough to mean
			something. It names a place, a season, a farm and a trade route, and it does all of that in
			the time it takes to break the ice with the person across the table. Nobody has ever bonded
			over a slide deck.
		</p>
		<p class="text-base lg:text-lg leading-relaxed text-bone">
			This booklet treats a cocktail as the smallest usable unit of cultural fluency. Each of the
			three ingredients here carries a story that ends in a supply chain question your counterpart
			in Manila, Osaka or Kyoto is dealing with right now. The drink is the entry point. The story
			is what makes it memorable.
		</p>
		<p class="text-base lg:text-lg leading-relaxed text-bone">
			Every recipe comes in a version with alcohol, and every chapter closes with one that has
			none. Half the drinks in this book are zero proof. That is not a concession. Some of the
			best flavor work in Asian bars right now happens without a drop of spirit.
		</p>
	</section>

	<!-- Three ingredients, three lessons -->
	<section id="ingredients" class="mx-auto max-w-6xl xl:max-w-7xl px-6 py-20 lg:py-24 flex flex-col gap-10">
		<h2 class="font-label text-xs text-ash">Three ingredients, three lessons</h2>
		<div class="grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-3">
			{lessons.map((item) => (
				<div id={item.id} class:list={['bg-stone border-l-[3px] px-6 py-6 flex flex-col gap-3 scroll-mt-24', accentBorder[item.accent]]}>
					{/* The mark takes the card's accent through `currentColor`, so the
					    color is declared once on the wrapper and the drawing follows
					    it. Pulled left by a hair because the yam and the bowl both
					    start with stroke rather than whitespace, and sitting flush
					    they read as indented against the labels below. */}
					<IngredientMark name={item.accent} size={76} class:list={['-ml-1', accentText[item.accent]]} />
					<p class:list={['font-label text-xs', accentText[item.accent]]}>{item.name}</p>
					<p class="font-serif text-xl text-bone">{item.lesson}</p>
					<p class="text-sm text-ash leading-relaxed">{item.blurb}</p>
					<a href={item.href} class:list={['font-label text-xs hover:opacity-75 transition-opacity mt-2', accentText[item.accent]]}>
						Read the chapter &rarr;
					</a>
				</div>
			))}
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- Comparison table -->
	<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl xl:max-w-7xl px-6 py-20 lg:py-24 flex flex-col gap-8">
		<h2 class="font-label text-xs text-ash">The short version</h2>

		<!-- Desktop / tablet: table, horizontal hairlines only -->
		<table class="hidden sm:table w-full text-left border-collapse">
			<thead>
				<tr class="border-b border-rule">
					<th class="py-3 pr-4 font-label text-xs text-mist font-medium"></th>
					<th class="py-3 px-4 font-label text-xs text-ube font-medium">Ube</th>
					<th class="py-3 px-4 font-label text-xs text-yuzu font-medium">Yuzu</th>
					<th class="py-3 pl-4 font-label text-xs text-matcha font-medium">Matcha</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				{comparisonRows.map((row) => (
					<tr class="border-b border-rule">
						<th scope="row" class="py-4 pr-4 text-sm text-ash font-normal text-left align-top">{row.label}</th>
						{row.values.map((value) => (
							<td class="py-4 px-4 text-sm text-bone align-top">{value}</td>
						))}
					</tr>
				))}
			</tbody>
		</table>

		<!-- Mobile: stacked, one block per ingredient -->
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-10 sm:hidden">
			{(['Ube', 'Yuzu', 'Matcha'] as const).map((name, colIndex) => (
				<div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
					<p class:list={['font-label text-xs', [accentText.ube, accentText.yuzu, accentText.matcha][colIndex]]}>{name}</p>
					<dl class="flex flex-col gap-3">
						{comparisonRows.map((row) => (
							<div class="border-b border-rule pb-3 flex flex-col gap-1">
								<dt class="text-xs text-mist">{row.label}</dt>
								<dd class="text-sm text-bone">{row.values[colIndex]}</dd>
							</div>
						))}
					</dl>
				</div>
			))}
		</div>
	</section>

	<Hairline />

	<!-- Closing band -->
	<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl xl:max-w-7xl px-6 py-20 lg:py-24 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:justify-between gap-8">
		<p class="font-serif text-xl text-bone max-w-md">
			Six cocktails, three chapters, one argument about how origin, value and control interact
			across Asian trade.
		</p>
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-6">
			{/* The recipes link steps back to plain text here. Two brass elements
			    side by side would compete, and the booklet is the ask. */}
			<a href="/recipes" class="font-label text-xs text-ash hover:text-brass transition-colors">
				See the recipes &rarr;
			</a>
			<BookletDownload label="Download the booklet" />
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
